## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash maps, deques, and heaps to implement a sliding-window top-K feature in real time.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window with per-player aggregates
- Lazy deletions in a heap to avoid frequent rebalancing
- Expiration management via per-player deques
- Trade-offs between memory usage and update/query latency

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map();
    this.heap = [];
  }

  addEvent(pid, delta, ts) {
    const record = this.map.get(pid) || { sum: 0, deque: [] };
    record.deque.push({ ts, delta });
    record.sum += delta;

    while (record.deque.length && record.deque[0].ts < ts - this.W) {
      record.sum -= record.deque.shift().delta;
    }

    this.map.set(pid, record);
    this.heap.push({ sum: record.sum, pid });
  }

  topK(now, K) {
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < K; i++) {
      while (this.heap.length) {
        const top = this.heap[0];
        const record = this.map.get(top.pid);
        if (!record || record.sum !== top.sum) {
          heapPop(this.heap);
        } else {
          results.push(this.heapPop(this.heap).pid);
          break;
        }
      }
    }
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Data Structure Overview
| Component | Purpose | Complexity |
|-----------|---------|-------------|
| Hash Map | O(1) lookup per player | O(n) memory |
| Per-player Deque | Track event history, expire old entries | O(1) amortized push/shift |
| Max-Heap | Maintain top players efficiently | O(log n) push, O(1) peek |

## Time & Space Complexity
- **addEvent**: O(log n) amortized—each event enters and leaves the deque at most once
- **topK**: O(K log n)—each invalid entry is popped at most once across all queries
- **Memory**: O(n) for the hash map and deques; O(n) worst-case for the heap

## Follow-up Questions
- How to tighten the lazy deletion cost when the heap grows large?
- Can a segment tree or Fenwick tree provide stricter time guarantees?
- How would you handle concurrent updates from multiple streams?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses augmenting a Trie to answer prefix-based queries with dynamic weights while maintaining efficient updates. Encourages careful handling of edge cases and update semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Trie augmentation with maxSubtreeWeight; non-decreasing weight updates.
- O(len(word)) insert and O(len(s)) query.
- Edge cases: empty word input, overlapping prefixes, no matches.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.wordWeight = 0;
    this.hasWord = false;
    this.maxSubtreeWeight = 0;
  }
}
class PrefixWeightTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new Node(); }
  insert(word, weight) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new Node());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    if (!node.hasWord || weight > node.wordWeight) {
      node.hasWord = true;
      node.wordWeight = weight;
    }
    // update maxSubtreeWeight along path (omitted detailed propagation for brevity)
  }
  queryLongestPrefix(s) {
    let node = this.root;
    let bestLen = 0, bestWeight = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
      const c = s[i];
      if (!node.next.has(c)) break;
      node = node.next.get(c);
      if (node.maxSubtreeWeight > 0) {
        bestLen = i + 1;
        bestWeight = node.maxSubtreeWeight;
      }
    }
    return [bestLen, bestWeight];
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests fundamental graph traversal algorithms, BFS implementation, and pathfinding skills - critical for autonomous vehicle navigation systems and route planning applications.

## Expected Answer
The candidate should implement BFS using a queue, maintain a visited set to avoid cycles, track distances from the start node, and handle edge cases such as when start equals target, disconnected graphs, or empty input. They should also explain why BFS guarantees the shortest path in unweighted graphs.

## Code Example
```python
from collections import deque

def shortest_path(graph, start, end):
    if start == end:
        return 0

    queue = deque([(start, 0)])
    visited = {start}

    while queue:
        node, distance = queue.popleft()

        for neighbor in graph.get(node, []):
            if neighbor == end:
                return distance + 1
            if neighbor not in visited:
                visited.add(neighbor)
                queue.append((neighbor, distance + 1))

    return -1
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design algorithms with provable performance guarantees, handle pathological inputs gracefully, and understand trade-offs between different sorting approaches. It's particularly relevant for large-scale systems where worst-case behavior impacts reliability.

## Key Concepts
- **Introsort**: Hybrid algorithm combining quicksort's average-case efficiency with heapsort's worst-case guarantees
- **3-way partitioning**: Dutch national flag algorithm that efficiently handles duplicate keys
- **Depth monitoring**: Switches to heapsort when recursion depth exceeds 2⌊log₂(n)⌋ to maintain O(n log n) worst-case
- **Space complexity**: O(log n) stack space through tail-call optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n || 1));

  function sort(left, right, depth) {
    if (left >= right) return;

    if (depth === 0) {
      heapSort(arr, left, right);
      return;
    }

    const [lt, gt] = threeWayPartition(arr, left, right);

    // Sort smaller partition first (tail recursion optimization)
    if (lt - left < right - gt) {
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
    } else {
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
    }
  }

  sort(0, n - 1, maxDepth);
  return arr;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a clean DP formulation for segmentation with per-placement costs and wildcards. It also probes backtracking and memoization versus tabulation choices under a dictionary constraint.

## Key Concepts
- DP on string positions with a dictionary
- Memoization vs bottom-up tabulation
- Backtracking reconstruction of segmentation
- Handling wildcards and per-placement costs

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCost(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(null);
  const choice = new Array(n).fill(null);

  function costAt(i, w) {
    let cost = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < w.length; j++) {
      if (s[i+j] !== '?' && s[i+j] !== w[j]) {
        cost++;
      }
    }
    return cost;
  }

  function dp(i) {
    if (i === n) return 0;
    if (memo[i] !== null) return memo[i];

    let best = Infinity;
    let bestWord = null;

    for (const w of dict) {
      if (i + w.length <= n) {
        const c = costAt(i, w) + dp(i + w.length);
        if (c < best) {
          best = c;
          bestWord = w;
        }
      }
    }

    memo[i] = best;
    choice[i] = bestWord;
    return best;
  }

  const minCost = dp(0);
  if (minCost === Infinity) return -1;

  // Reconstruct segmentation
  const words = [];
  let i = 0;
  while (i < n) {
    words.push(choice[i]);
    i += choice[i].length;
  }

  return { cost: minCost, words };
}
```

## Complexity
Time: O(n × sum|w|) where sum|w| is total length of all dictionary words
Space: O(n) for memoization and choice arrays

